Confirmed: the single OnboardingScreen is the boarding screen. The bug: user can fling the PageView past page 2 (provider step) while the gate is active, reach the final page, and press "Open Fa" — authorizing the app without a provider/model configured.

The fix: block forward swiping past the provider page while the gate is active. Options:
1. `physics: NeverScrollableScrollPhysics()` when gated — but that also blocks swiping back from page 1 to page 0. Actually when gated on page 1, user may want to swipe back to page 0. But a simpler approach: disable swiping entirely whenever the gate is active. Hmm — the gate only matters when the user is on or before page 1. If the user is on page 1 and gated, swiping back to 0 is fine, forward is not.

Best approach: a custom ScrollPhysics that prevents forward scrolling while gated. Something like:

```dart
class _GatePhysics extends ScrollPhysics {
  const _GatePhysics({required this.gated, super.parent});
  final bool gated;
  @override
  _GatePhysics applyTo(ScrollPhysics? ancestor) => _GatePhysics(gated: gated, parent: buildParent(ancestor));
  @override
  double applyPhysicsToUserOffset(ScrollPosition position, double offset) {
    if (gated && offset < 0) return 0; // block forward scroll
    return super.applyPhysicsToUserOffset(position, offset);
  }
```

Wait, applyPhysicsToUserOffset handles drag deltas; flings (velocity) are handled by createBallisticSimulation — the viewport's scroll activity converts velocity to a ballistic simulation that changes the pixels directly via goBallistic... Actually for fling gestures, applyPhysicsToUserOffset isn't called; the ScrollPosition goes ballistic via beginActivity → the viewport calls physics.createBallisticSimulation. Blocking that requires createBallisticSimulation to clamp.

Simpler and robust: use NeverScrollableScrollPhysics while on the gated page. Downside: while gated and on page 1, the user can't swipe back to page 0 — but they can press Back (onBack is enabled when _page > 0). Acceptable? The ticket says "disable swiping". Simplest per ticket.

But consider also: even with gate active and NeverScrollableScrollPhysics, once on page 0, user can't swipe to page 1 either — they'd use Continue. Hmm, that changes UX: gate is active even on page 0 (since provider not configured). Blocking swipe from page 0 to page 1 with physics would prevent navigating to page 1 at all via swipe, but Continue button still works. That might actually be desirable? No — user needs to reach page 2 to configure the provider. Continue button on page 0 goes _next via controller which works with NeverScrollableScrollPhysics? PageController.nextPage uses animateToPage which programmatically sets page — physics doesn't block programmatic jumps (physics only affects user gestures). Yes, NeverScrollableScrollPhysics blocks user scrolling but programmatic animateTo works.

So simplest fix: `physics: _providerGateActive ? const NeverScrollableScrollPhysics() : null`. But while gated, even swiping back is blocked. Alternative: only block when on page 1 and gated? When user is on page 0 gated — swipe to page 1 is fine (that's forward toward the mandatory step). Once on page 1 gated, block forward swipe but allow backward.

A cleaner approach many apps use: physics = NeverScrollableScrollPhysics() always for gated flow... Let me think about the best behavior:

- Gate active, page 0: user should be able to swipe forward to page 1 (provider). Blocking this is slightly annoying but Continue works.
- Gate active, page 1: forward swipe must be blocked; backward swipe to 0 is nice-to-have.

Custom physics allowing only backward movement while gated on page 1:

```dart
class _NoForwardSwipePhysics extends ScrollPhysics {
  const _NoForwardSwipePhysics({super.parent});
  @override
  _NoForwardSwipePhysics applyTo(ScrollPhysics? ancestor) =>
      _NoForwardSwipePhysics(parent: buildParent(ancestor));
  @override
  double applyPhysicsToUserOffset(ScrollPosition position, double offset) =>
      offset < 0 ? 0 : super.applyPhysicsToUserOffset(position, offset);
}
```

But fling: when user flings forward on page 1, the ScrollPosition goes ballistic — applyPhysicsToUserOffset isn't consulted; createBallisticSimulation default returns a simulation based on velocity that would scroll forward. To block ballistic forward motion, override createBallisticSimulation? Actually the easier robust path: clamp in physics via `shouldAcceptUserOffset`? There's no such API.

Alternative well-known approach: NeverScrollableScrollPhysics while on gated page. This blocks flings too since user gestures can't initiate scrolling at all. Back navigation via Back button works. I'll apply: `physics: gated ? const NeverScrollableScrollPhysics() : null` where gated = _page == 1 && _providerGateActive. That way page 0 swipe still works (user can swipe into the provider page), and once on the gated provider page all swiping is disabled. That's precise and minimal.

Wait, but when on page 0 and gate active, swiping forward to page 1 works — then on page 1 swipe disabled. Good. Also when gate becomes inactive (provider configured), physics reverts to normal.

Edge: while on gated page 1, can the user still programmatically go back? Yes, Back button calls _pc.previousPage.

Also desktop: mouse drag scrolling on PageView — blocked too, good.

Now check the "Open Fa" button on last page — not reachable by swipe when gated. 

TDD: write a failing test first:

Test: 'swiping past the mandatory provider step is blocked' — pump with registry, initialPage 1, fling forward, expect still on 'Choose how Fa thinks.'. Also test that after configuring provider, swipe works.

